[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Source NAT on the border router r1 (iptables NAT/POSTROUTING). Mechanism: PC1’s traffic to public IPs is forwarded through the campus and reaches r1, but leaves r1 toward ISPA with an RFC1918 source (10.x). ISPA has no route back to 10.x, so replies are dropped. That makes all outbound TCP connections from clients time out, while r1 itself can reach the Internet (its packets have public source IPs). Localization: Device r1, WAN-facing interface eth2 (203.0.113.2) toward ISPA. Evidence: 
- PC1 has a valid default (10.1.0.1) and can reach the core and r1 (pc1→10.0.0.33 OK). 
- From r1, ping to ISPA and public IPs works (203.0.113.1, 216.239.35.4, 66.235.200.145: all OK), proving the ISP path is up. 
- From PC1, ping to those same public/ISP IPs fails (100% loss), consistent with missing NAT. 
- r1 iptables shows no MASQUERADE/SNAT in POSTROUTING (iptables -t nat -vnL), and ip_forward=1, so forwarding is on but no translation is occurring. 
- ISPA’s routing table has no routes to 10.0.0.0/8, so replies to untranslated 10.x sources would be dropped.

Root cause: The NAT configuration on r1 was removed/omitted (likely during maintenance), leaving no SNAT/MASQUERADE for internal subnets.

Corrective fix: Reintroduce NAT on r1, e.g., add iptables -t nat -A POSTROUTING -s 10.0.0.0/8 -o eth2 -j MASQUERADE (and similarly for eth3 if used), and persist the rule. After this, client TCP sessions to public Internet should succeed without ISP action. === END ===